Baginski Software DotEnv
========================

Type-safe environment variable validation for JavaScript runtimes.

The library provides tools to define, load, and validate environment variables
with TypeScript type inference and runtime validation.

It consists of two modules:

* core: Defines environment schemas, infers variables types, and controls
  access to server, client, and shared variables.

* loader: Loads and parses environment variables from .env files.

The library is independent of specific validation libraries and JavaScript runtimes.
It is designed to work with compatible validators and modern JavaScript environments.

Quick Start
-----------

* Install with Bun: bun add @baginskisoftware/dotenv
* Install with npm: npm install @baginskisoftware/dotenv
* Source code: git@github:baginskisoftware/dotenv
* Validation interoperability: https://standardschema.dev/

Basic Usage
-----------

Define the environment schema and specify its source.

The following example uses Zod to define validation schemas.
Zod is optional; other compatible validation libraries can be used instead.


# import { defineEnvironment } from "@baginskisoftware/dotenv";
# import { loadEnvFirst } from "@baginskisoftware/dotenv/loader";
# import z from "zod";
#
# export const Environment = defineEnvironment({
#   runtimeEnv: loadEnvFirst([".env.local", ".env"]),
#   server: {
#     DATABASE_URL: z.url(),
#     PORT: z.coerce.number().int().min(1025),
#   },
# })();

The returned factorty processes and validates the environment when invoked.

Environment Configuration
-------------------------

The "runtimeEnv" property defines the source of environment variables.
It accepts an environment object or the result of a loader function.

Use "process.env" when variables are already available in the runtime.

# import { defineEnvironment } from "@baginskisoftware/dotenv";
#
# export const Environment = defineEnvironment({
#   runtimeEnv: process.env,
#   server: { ... },
# });

Use "loadEnv" to load variables from a specific file.

# import { loadEnv } from "@baginskisoftware/dotenv/loader";
# const runtimeEnv = loadEnv(".env.local");

Use "loadEnvFirst" to select the first applicable source.

# import { loadEnvFirst } from "@baginskisoftware/dotenv/loader";
# const runtimeEnv = loadEnvFirst([".env.local", ".env"]);

The resulting environment source can be passed to "defineEnvironment"
through the "runtimeEnv" property.

Environment Schemas
-------------------

The core module defines the environment variables required by an application.
Schemas can separate variables into three categories:

* server: Variables intended for server-side use.
* client: Variables intended for client-side use.
* shared: Variables accessible to both environments.

The core module uses schema definitions to infer variable types and control
access to declared environment variables.

Validation is delegated to compatible validation libraries rather than being
tied to a specific implementation.

Validation Libraries
--------------------

The libraries is agnostic of validation libraries.

Any validator compatible with the interfaces supported by "core" can be used to
define environment schemas.

Standard Schema provides a common interfaces for interoperability between
validation libraries: https://standardschema.dev/

Examples of compatible validation libraries include:

* Valibot: https://valibot.dev/
* ArkType: https://arktype.io/
* Zod: https://zod.dev/

Zod is used throughout this document for demonstration purposes only.
It is not a required dependency of the library.

Applications may use another compatible validator without changing the
library's underlying architecture.

Compatibility depends on the validator implementing the interface required by
the version of the library being used.

Environment Processing
----------------------

"defineEnvironment" returns a factory.
Environment processing begins when the factory is invoked.

This allows applications to control when environment processing occurs and avoid
unnecessary work during module initialization.

The factory supports complete and selective environment construction.

# const FullEnvironment = BaseEnvironment();
#
# const FilteredEnvironment = BaseEnvironment([
#   "DATABASE_URL",
#   "PORT",
# ]);

Selective construction is useful when an application requires only a subset of
its declared environment variables.

Error Handling
--------------

The "core" module supports custom handlers for validation failures and invalid
environment variable access.

* onValidationError: Handles validation issues.
* onInvalidAccess: Handles access to unavailable variables.

These handlers allow applications to define their own error behavior without
requiring a specific error-reporting implementation.

Runtime Compatibility
---------------------

The library is designed for modern JavaScript runtimes.
It does not require a specific runtime as its underlying platform.

The available behavior depends on the environment source and the capabilities
provided by the selected runtime.

The "core" module handles environment definitions and validation, while the loader
module provides environment variable loading.

Runtime-specific APIs should be used only when available in the target environment.

Design Principles
-----------------

The project follows a simple and focused design philosophy:

* Simplicity: Keep the implementation straightforward.
* Portability: Support modern JavaScript runtimes.
* Interoperability: Avoid unnecessary dependence on specific validation libraries.
* Type safety: Preserve TypeScript inference.
* Minimalism: Avoid unnecessary dependencies and runtime work.
* Extensibility: Allow applications to choose compatible tools.

Complexity should be introduced only when it provides a clear, practical benefit.

